================= Bug Report (1/1) ==================
## Source: GitHub Issue
## URL: https://github.com/quickjs-ng/quickjs/issues/1302
## Description:
Issue: quickjs-ng/quickjs#1302
Title: Heap use-after-free in js_atomics_op
State: closed
Created by: Mcsky23
Created at: 2026-01-05

Issue Body:
A heap use-after-free vulnerability exists in the Atomics operations of quickjs-ng. The function `js_atomics_get_ptr()` caches a raw pointer into the ArrayBuffer's heap-allocated backing store. After caching, `JS_ToUint32(ctx, &v32, argv[2])` is called to convert the value argument. If the value argument has a custom `valueOf()` method, arbitrary JS code runs -- that JS code can call `rab.resize(0)` on a resizable ArrayBuffer, which frees and reallocates the buffer memory via `js_realloc`. The cached pointer is now dangling. When the atomic operation (add/sub/and/or/xor/exchange/compareExchange) proceeds, it writes to freed memory.

Affected functions: `Atomics.add`, `Atomics.sub`, `Atomics.and`, `Atomics.or`, `Atomics.xor`, `Atomics.exchange`, `Atomics.compareExchange` (all go through `js_atomics_op`), plus `Atomics.store`, `Atomics.wait`, `Atomics.notify`.

Affected versions: All versions of quickjs-ng up to and including v0.11.0.

PoC:
```javascript
const rab = new ArrayBuffer(1024, { maxByteLength: 1024 * 1024 });
const i32 = new Int32Array(rab);
const evil = {
    valueOf: () => {
        rab.resize(0);
        return 123;
    }
};
Atomics.add(i32, 0, evil);
```

When built with AddressSanitizer, this produces:
```
ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free on address ... in js_atomics_op
WRITE of size 4
```

Credit: Vlad Ionut Seba (Mcsky23)
